<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andi Djemma</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1965">1965</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andi Djemma was the datu, or king, of Luwu in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> from 1935. He declared Luwu's support for the Indonesian Republic in 1945 and led resistance to the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> until his capture in 1946. He was named a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-hero-of-indonesia">National Hero of Indonesia</a> in 2002.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andi Djemma was born in Palopo on 15 January 1901 into the royal family of Luwu. He spent five years at an Inlandsche School, a native Indonesian school in Luwu, and was then taught informally at the royal court. His mother often took him to meetings between colonial officials and the kingdom's administrators, where his interest in Indonesian nationalism grew. He became a district official in 1919 and served in districts such as Ngapa and Ware, but was dismissed in 1931 for alleged links to a nationalist organisation that he was said to be funding in secret.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father's death he was one of twelve candidates for the throne, of whom three remained after the royal council's review. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> government was uneasy about him, but he was chosen because his supporters threatened conflict if he was passed over.</p><p>After the Japanese occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> and the Proclamation of Indonesian Independence, he consulted Sam Ratulangi, governor of the new province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a>, and decided to back the republic. He formed a paramilitary group called Soekarno Moeda (Young Sukarno), later led by his son Andi Makkalau, which raided Japanese positions for weapons, and he persuaded other traditional rulers to refuse cooperation with the Netherlands Indies Civil Administration.</p><p>After soldiers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army">Royal Netherlands East Indies Army</a> destroyed a mosque, he ordered all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> officials on 21 January 1946 to leave Luwu within 48 hours. Fighting began on 23 January, with militias also attacking Australian troops, and the South Sulawesi campaign of 1946–1947 followed. Arrested on 3 July 1946, he was exiled to Ternate and sentenced to death, a sentence cut to 25 years; he was released soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-indonesian-round-table-conference">Dutch–Indonesian Round Table Conference</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He remained king of Luwu and a civil servant until his death on 23 February 1965. He received the Satyalacana Karya in 1964. Besides his title of National Hero, a street in Makassar, a university in Palopo and Andi Jemma Airport bear his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andi Djemma” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its datu <b>Andi Djemma</b> backed the Indonesian Republic in 1945 to 1946 and is now a National Hero of Indonesia.</blockquote><a href="/state/luwu-kingdom">Luwu Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Palopo, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3,120.2&amp;year=1942" aria-label="Palopo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Andi Djemma"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5017 5256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5017 5256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3,120.2&amp;year=1942">What was here in 1942? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
